<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Pale of Calais</h1><p class="st-meta">1347 CE – 1558 CE · capital Calais</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">England's last foothold in mainland France, a fortified port and wool staple held for two centuries after Edward III's siege of 1346–1347.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After his victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Crécy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III</a> besieged Calais for eleven months and took it in 1347; the story of the six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> who offered their lives is famous. The English expelled much of the population and settled the town with English colonists, adding a surrounding district of marsh and forts, the Pale.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Calais was garrisoned by the largest standing force of the English crown. From 1363 it hosted the Staple, through which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England's</a> raw wool exports passed, and the Merchants of the Staple funded its defence. From 1536 it sent members to the English Parliament. It was a base for English armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for two centuries.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In January 1558, during a war in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Francis, duke of Guise, captured Calais in a surprise winter attack. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-i-of-england">Mary I</a> reportedly said that Calais would be found written on her heart. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-pale">The Pale</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pale-of-calais.jpg" alt="Pale of Calais" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Original: traditional Vector: Nicholas Shanks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_England.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1347 CE – 1558 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th